{
 "id": "017-cok-kapan-tutun",
 "lang": "en",
 "title": "Drop, Cover, Hold On!",
 "theme": "Earthquake drill and disaster preparedness",
 "blurb": "When the drill bell rings, Zeynep gets flustered, but she remembers the turtle and takes a deep breath: Drop, cover, hold on! Elif shows her own way in her wheelchair: Lock, cover, hold on.",
 "voiceNames": {
  "zeynep": "Zeynep",
  "elif": "Elif",
  "ogretmen": "Miss Nur",
  "anne": "Mum (Zeynep's mum)",
  "baba": "Dad (Zeynep's dad)"
 },
 "pages": [
  {
   "text": "Everyone gathered on the rug. Miss Nur asked: “What does a turtle do when it sees danger?” Zeynep put her hand up. “It pulls its head into its shell! And waits calmly.”",
   "hits": {
    "ogretmen": {
     "say": "What does a turtle do? Does anyone know?"
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "I know! It hides in its shell!"
    },
    "elif": {
     "say": "Its shell is its safe little home!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Zeynep!",
   "ask": "Have you ever seen a turtle? What did it do when it pulled into its shell?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Miss Nur smiled: “Today we're having an earthquake drill. A drill means practice. When the bell rings, we'll be turtles: Drop, cover, hold on!” Elif showed them right away. “First I lock my wheels, click! Then I cover and hold on.”",
   "hits": {
    "elif": {
     "say": "Click! My wheels are locked."
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "I'll be a turtle too!"
    },
    "ogretmen": {
     "say": "Cover means tuck your head into your shell!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Elif!",
   "ask": "Why do you think Elif locks her wheels first?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Zeynep was playing with blocks when the bell rang: ding, ding, ding! Zeynep got all flustered. Her heart went thump, thump. Her feet wanted to run to the door.",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "My heart is going thump, thump!"
    },
    "ogretmen": {
     "say": "The drill has started, turtles!"
    }
   },
   "predict": {
    "q": "What do you think Zeynep will do?",
    "res": "Both are good ideas! Let's see.",
    "opts": [
     {
      "l": "Take a deep breath"
     },
     {
      "l": "Look at her teacher"
     }
    ]
   },
   "hint": "Touch Zeynep!",
   "ask": "Has your heart ever gone thump, thump? What did you do then?",
   "cues": [
    {
     "w": 19,
     "fx": "kalp"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Zeynep looked at her teacher and remembered the turtle. She took a deep breath: hhhhh… fwooo. Drop! She went down on her knees. Cover! She crawled under the table and covered her head and neck with her arms. Hold on! She held the table leg. Elif locked her wheels too: click! She covered, and held on.",
   "mech": {
    "say": "Drop! Cover! Hold on!",
    "done": "The table became Zeynep's shell!",
    "msg": "She's in her shell!"
   },
   "hits": {
    "elif": {
     "say": "Lock, cover, hold on!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Zeynep three times!",
   "ask": "Drop, cover, hold on… Can you try it now, right where you're sitting?"
  },
  {
   "text": "The drill was over, and the turtles came out of their shells. Miss Nur put water and plasters in the earthquake bag. “What would you add?” Zeynep brought a scarf and a toothbrush. Elif laughed: “And a teddy bear to cuddle!”",
   "mech": {
    "done": "The bag is ready! Everyone did their job.",
    "msg": "The bag is ready!"
   },
   "hits": {
    "ogretmen": {
     "say": "Our bag will always stay by the door."
    },
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "A scarf keeps us warm!"
    },
    "elif": {
     "say": "Teddy is ready with us too!"
    },
    "su": {
     "say": "Water is very important!"
    },
    "bant": {
     "say": "The plasters are ready too!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Put the things in the bag!",
   "ask": "What would you like to put in this bag? Why?"
  },
  {
   "text": "That evening, Zeynep taught her family the turtle game. “Drop, cover, hold on!” Dad dropped too, but he didn't fit under the table! They all giggled: tee-hee-hee! Zeynep felt calm inside. Now she knew just what to do.",
   "hits": {
    "zeynep": {
     "say": "Drop, cover, hold on!"
    },
    "baba": {
     "say": "I'm a great big turtle!"
    },
    "anne": {
     "say": "Teacher Zeynep, it's my turn!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch Dad!",
   "ask": "If Dad doesn't fit under the table, what can he do?",
   "cues": [
    {
     "w": 25,
     "fx": "kahkaha"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "HSAB.11 (learning outcome): Protecting oneself from danger and accidents — knows what to do in a danger, accident or disaster situation: carries out the “Drop, cover, hold on” steps in order during the drill.",
   "HSAB.1 (learning outcome): Using gross motor skills effectively in different settings and physical activities — a) performs locomotor movements in different settings and conditions (dropping down, crawling); b) performs balance movements suited to the activity (holding on).",
   "SDB1.2 Self-Regulation: notices worry in her body (a thumping heart) and calms down with a deep breath. E1.5 Self-Confidence: feels safe because she knows what to do.",
   "D16 Responsibility: takes a job when packing the class earthquake bag and shares what she has learned with her family."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Look at a photo of a turtle together: “What does a turtle do when it gets startled?” Talk about how its shell keeps it safe.",
   "Say from the start that a drill is a “practice game” and that there is no real danger. The story only shows the drill; there is no shaking and no damage.",
   "You can read it in the week of Disaster Education Preparedness Day (12 November, unverified) or before your school's own drill."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 2, highlight Elif's way: wheelchair users say “Lock, cover, hold on” (the Turkish phrase is unverified; lock the wheels, lean forward, cover your head and neck with your arms, hold on to your chair). Everyone's safe way can be a little different.",
   "Pause on page 3: “Why is Zeynep's heart thumping? How would you feel?” Name the feeling; say that feeling flustered is normal.",
   "On page 4, do the movements together where you are sitting as you say “Drop! Cover! Hold on!”"
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Remember the order together: what did we do first, what next, and what last? Show it with your bodies.",
   "Try “turtle breathing”: in slowly through the nose, out slowly through the mouth. Talk about when it helps.",
   "Go back to the page where Dad doesn't fit under the table: what can we do if there is no table? (Drop to the floor away from windows and things that could fall over, and cover your head and neck with your arms.)"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Turtle Drill",
   "sure": "15 min",
   "materyal": "Sturdy children's tables, a hand bell or a soft signal sound, an empty backpack, a water bottle, plasters, a scarf, a small toy",
   "adim": [
    "Be turtles on the rug: tuck your head between your arms and “pull into your shell”, breathing in and out slowly.",
    "With a soft bell sound, practise the “Drop, cover, hold on” steps once under the tables; children who use a wheelchair lock the wheels, lean forward and cover their head and neck with their arms. Do not use a siren or a loud noise.",
    "Pack the class bag together: each child puts in one item and says what it is for.",
    "Reflection circle: “How did you feel when the bell rang? What calmed you down?”"
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "How did Zeynep feel when the bell rang? What did she do next?",
   "Drop, cover, hold on: can you show me?",
   "Which sturdy table could be our “shell” at home?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Our shell at home",
   "d": "Choose a sturdy table together at home and try “Drop, cover, hold on” once, like a game. Then add a small toy or book your child chooses to your family bag. Keep the game short and cheerful; if your child doesn't want to, don't push."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Drop, Cover, Hold On!”: Zeynep gets flustered during the drill, then takes a deep breath like a turtle and calms down; Elif shows her own way in her wheelchair. The story only shows a drill; there is no shaking, no damage and no scary sound. Earthquakes can carry difficult memories for some children and families: answer your child's questions briefly and calmly, and accept their feelings (“It's very normal to feel flustered. I'm right here with you.”). If the worry goes on for a long time or affects sleep or play, you can talk to our school counsellor."
 },
